\section{Optimization Changes Granularity and Execution Plan}

Alternative-language graphs expose transformations beyond scalar instruction selection. Fusion combines adjacent computations; fission splits or replicates one computation; scheduling orders firings; buffering chooses storage between them; vectorization groups elements; incrementalization reuses prior results after a small change. These transformations interact. Fusing actors may remove a FIFO but enlarge state, reduce parallelism, and change the best schedule. Brunet's work on dynamic dataflow highlights buffer sizing and design-space exploration as first-class concerns rather than cleanup after code generation.

A declarative pipeline specification might be:

\begin{verbatim}
pipeline {
  map(scale 2.0)
  map(clamp 0.0 1.0)
  window(size=4, step=1)
  reduce(sum)
}
\end{verbatim}

The two maps are promising fusion candidates. The sliding window is stateful across items; duplicating it requires partition halos or replicated state. The reduction's associativity and numerical rules determine vectorization freedom.

\section{Attach Preconditions and Cost to Every Rewrite}

Optimization IR should retain graph regions, rates, shapes, state partitions, effects, order, buffer capacities, and target costs. A transform produces a new graph plus a correspondence between old and new observations. Preconditions are machine-checkable facts where possible. Profitability is separate: a legal fusion can still worsen cache behavior or parallel throughput.

\Cref{fig:visual-380} shows a typical tradeoff between fewer buffers and less pipeline parallelism.

