Output artifacts include the optimized graph, static or dynamic schedule, buffer and memory plan, vector kernels, incremental cache schema, target code, and a transformation record. Performance profiles are versioned inputs: stale profiles must not silently certify semantics or current profitability.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Search Plans While Revalidating Semantics}

\begin{verbatim}
worklist := {verified baseline graph}
while budget remains:
    candidate := choose graph and transform
    obligations := candidate.transform.preconditions(graph.contracts)
    if prove(obligations):
        rewritten := apply(candidate)
        verify rates, shapes, effects, state, and token order
        estimate throughput, latency, memory, and recomputation cost
        keep nondominated verified plans
select plan satisfying target constraints and emit transformation record
\end{verbatim}

Fusion is legal for composable operations when boundary observations and state/effect order survive. Fission needs a partition of tokens and state, plus an order-preserving join where order matters. Scheduling must avoid underflow, overflow, and forbidden effect reordering. Buffer elimination requires direct handoff compatibility. Vectorization needs alignment, tail, alias, and numerical proofs. Incrementalization needs sound invalidation and versioned dependencies; otherwise stale answers are possible.

Validation combines local proof checks with end-to-end differential execution. Rate balance and occupancy simulation check stream plans; randomized shapes and aliases test vector kernels; edit sequences compare incremental and full recomputation; source and optimized runs compare values, errors, state, and ordered effects. Numerical tolerances are valid only when the source contract permits approximation. The transformation record lets a failure be bisected to the first pass whose claimed correspondence no longer holds.

\section{Fuse the Stateless Prefix Without Moving the Window}

Take inputs \(0.10,0.25,0.75,-0.10,0.40\) and use an idealized decimal calculation for illustration. Scaling then clamping produces \(0.20,0.50,1,0,0.80\). The two complete width-four windows sum to \(1.70\) and \(2.30\). A fused map may perform scale and clamp in one actor while preserving their order and numeric semantics. Moving clamp after the sum changes the result; likewise, emitting a partial window at startup changes the output rate at the boundary.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Candidate & First outputs & Decision \\
\hline
Original prefix and windows & \(1.70,2.30\) & Baseline \\
Fused scale then clamp & \(1.70,2.30\) & Check arithmetic and effects \\
Clamp summed window & \(1,1\) & Reject \\
Emit on every input & Extra prefix outputs & Reject \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
push_sample(x, ring):
    y := original_clamp(original_scale(x))
    if ring.is_full(): ring.drop_oldest()
    ring.append(y)
    if ring.count < 4: return NoOutput
    return Emit(sum_in_original_order(ring.oldest_first()))
\end{verbatim}

This eliminates an intermediate stream but retains four transformed values. Another candidate maintains a running sum and subtracts the evicted value. That rewrite changes the floating-point operation sequence and can behave differently for NaNs even after the offending sample leaves the window. If the numeric contract is exact integer arithmetic with checked overflow, prove the intermediate adds and subtracts are legal too. A small fixture with three input samples checks the prologue; a fourth sample tests the first output, and a fifth tests that eviction occurs before summation. Benchmark only candidates that survived these checks.

\paragraph{Measure latency as well as traffic.}
A fused actor can reduce intermediate writes but tie two scheduling
decisions together. Report the time to the first complete window
separately from steady-state throughput, and record the peak number
of queued inputs while the consumer is stalled. Otherwise a batching
optimization that looks faster in the aggregate may miss the source's
latency budget on short streams.
